            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of total and partial traumatic tears of the subscapularis tendon.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>348-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We treated surgically 16 shoulders with an isolated traumatic rupture of the subscapularis tendon over a six-year period. Nine patients had a total and seven a partial tear of the subscapularis tendon. Repair was undertaken through a small deltopectoral groove approach. The mean Constant score improved in total tears from 38.7 to 89.3 points (p = 0.003) and in partial tears from 50.7 to 87.9 points (p = 0.008). The total tears were significantly more improved by surgery than the partial tears (p = 0.001). The delay between trauma and surgery was inversely proportional to the improvement in the Constant score suggesting that early diagnosis and surgical repair improves outcome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
